{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QIntegerCanonical","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QIntegerCanonical","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mass.QHookDimension","lean:module:QOU.Mass.QHookDimensionBalanced","lean:module:QOU.Mass.QIntegerBarParity"],"declarations":34,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qFactBal_eq_pow_div_yTrivial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qFactBal_eq_pow_div_yTrivial","kind":"theorem","line":444,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L444-L454","doc":"**`[n]_q! = qⁿ / y_{(n)}(q)`** — the q-factorial *is* the inverse trivial\nMarkov weight, content-shifted.\n\nOnly `q ≠ 0` is needed — the content shift must be invertible.  A first draft also\ncarried `[n]_q! ≠ 0`, on the reasoning that the singular locus collapses the\nproduct; writing the proof showed that hypothesis is not used.  The identity\nsurvives `[n]_q! = 0`, because Lean's `x / 0 = 0` sends both sides to `0` there. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.yTrivial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.yTrivial","kind":"def","line":435,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L435-L442","doc":"**The content-shifted Markov weight of the trivial partition** `(n) ⊢ n`:\n`y_{(n)}(q) = qⁿ / [n]_q!`.\n\nThe corpus states this identification at `n = 3` in `f-bor-borromean-observable`\n(`y_{(3)}(q) := q³ / [3]_q!`), where it turns the Borromean observable into\n`F_Bor = |tr_M(β_Bor)| / y_{(3)}(q)`.  Named here so the relation to `qFactBal`\nis machine-checked rather than carried in prose. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qFactBal_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qFactBal_three","kind":"theorem","line":420,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L420-L433","doc":"**`[3]_q! = [2]_q · [3]_q`** — the identity that reframes a two-index product\nas a one-index q-factorial.\n\nThis is the whole content of the observation about `conj:magic-shell-coupling-kappa`'s\nnormaliser: what reads as \"the LR fusion of two magic-shell channels `V_2 ⊗ V_3`\"\nis `[3]_q!`, hence `q³ / y_{(3)}(q)` — the inverse Markov weight of the trivial\npartition `(3) ⊢ 3`.  A rule for *two* channel indices has many readings that\nagree at `A = 3`; a q-factorial has *one* index, so the question of how the\nnormaliser continues off `A = 3` is correspondingly narrower. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_one_of_ne_sing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_one_of_ne_sing","kind":"theorem","line":412,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L412-L418","doc":"`[1]_q = 1` — away from the singular locus, where it is `0/0`.\n\nThe hypothesis is the same `q − q⁻¹ ≠ 0` that `qIntBalClSing` guards, and it is\nnot removable: at `q = ±1` (and at `q = 0`, via Lean's `0⁻¹ = 0`) the quotient is\n`0/0 = 0`, so `[1]_q = 0` there and every q-factorial below collapses. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qFactBal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qFactBal","kind":"def","line":402,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L402-L410","doc":"**The canonical q-factorial** `[n]_q! = ∏_{i=1}^{n} [i]_q`, balanced convention.\n\nWorth having as a named object rather than as ad-hoc bracket products: the corpus\nalready carries `[3]_q!` in two load-bearing places — the Borromean observable\n`F_Bor = |tr_M(β_Bor)| · [3]_q! / q³`, and the q-Schur Markov weight of the\ntrivial partition, `y_{(3)}(q) := q³ / [3]_q!`.  A bracket product written out\nlong-hand hides that identification. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntGeoCl_eq_closed_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntGeoCl_eq_closed_form","kind":"theorem","line":384,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L384-L394","doc":"**Closed form of the guarded geometric bracket.**\n\n`qIntGeo` is the `Finset.sum`, but every guarded-geometric site in the corpus\nwrites the closed form `(qⁿ − 1)/(q − 1)`.  Migrating such a site onto\n`qIntGeoCl` therefore replaces the body by a *propositionally* — not\ndefinitionally — equal one, and a clean compile does **not** by itself witness\nthat: it only shows nothing downstream constrained the body.  This lemma is the\nmissing witness, so the substitution is checked rather than assumed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_three","kind":"theorem","line":378,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L378-L382","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_two","kind":"theorem","line":374,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L374-L376","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalClSing_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalClSing_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":357,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L357-L359","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalCl_neg_one_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalCl_neg_one_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":343,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L343-L355","doc":"The separating instance, stated so the difference is a theorem and not a\nremark: at `q = −1` the one-sided guard falls through to `0`, the two-sided one\nreturns `n`.\n\n**The hypothesis is not boilerplate.** In characteristic `2` we have `−1 = 1`,\nso `qIntBalCl (−1) n = qIntBalCl 1 n = n` and this statement is *false* — the\ntwo guarded forms coincide there because the two-point locus `{1, −1}` has\ncollapsed to one point.  `(-1 : R) ≠ 1` says exactly \"the characteristic is not\n`2`\", which is what makes the one-sided guard genuinely one-sided. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalClSing_eq_qIntBalCl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalClSing_eq_qIntBalCl","kind":"theorem","line":321,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L321-L341","doc":"**Exactly where the two guarded forms agree.**  Off `q ∈ {0, −1}` they are\nthe same function; on those two points they differ (`qIntBalCl` falls through to\n`qIntBal`, which is `0` there, while `qIntBalClSing` returns `n`).  This is the\nlemma that makes \"one-sided vs two-sided guard is a behaviour change\" checkable\nrather than asserted — the same service `qIntBal_one_eq_zero` performs for\nguarded vs unguarded. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalClSing_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalClSing_one","kind":"theorem","line":318,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L318-L319","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalClSing_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalClSing_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":312,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L312-L316","doc":"Away from the singular locus the guard is invisible — the bridge a\nsingular-locus-guarded site needs to reuse any unguarded `qIntBal` lemma. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalClSing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalClSing","kind":"def","line":295,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L295-L310","doc":"**Singular-locus-guarded balanced q-integer**: `qIntBal` away from the whole\nlocus where the balanced denominator vanishes, and `n` on it.\n\nThis is the two-sided companion to `qIntBalCl`, and the form four corpus sites\nactually use.  Two honesty notes on the guard, since `q − q⁻¹ = 0` is *not* the\nsame set as `q = ±1`:\n\n* **`q = −1` is a genuine removable singularity** and `n` is its correct value,\n  which is why the guard is worth having: `qIntBalCl` returns `0` there.\n* **`q = 0` is caught too**, because Lean's `0⁻¹ = 0` makes `q − q⁻¹ = 0` hold\n  at zero.  The value `n` there is *not* a limit of anything — it is junk from a\n  junk input.  The guard is stated on the denominator rather than on `q = ±1`\n  because that is what the call sites wrote; nothing downstream evaluates at\n  `q = 0`, and the substrate sits at `q₀ ≈ 1.11`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_one_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_one_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":288,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L288-L292","doc":"The guard is not cosmetic: at `q = 1` the unguarded balanced form evaluates\nto `0` (Lean's `x / 0 = 0`), not to `n`.  This is the lemma that makes\n\"guarded → unguarded is a behaviour change\" checkable rather than asserted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntGeoCl_of_ne_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntGeoCl_of_ne_one","kind":"theorem","line":284,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L284-L286","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalCl_of_ne_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalCl_of_ne_one","kind":"theorem","line":278,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L278-L282","doc":"**Away from the singularity the guard is invisible** — the bridge a guarded\nsite needs in order to reuse any unguarded `qIntBal` lemma. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntGeoCl_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntGeoCl_one","kind":"theorem","line":275,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L275-L276","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalCl_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalCl_one","kind":"theorem","line":272,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L272-L273","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntGeoCl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntGeoCl","kind":"def","line":267,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L267-L270","doc":"**Classical-extended geometric q-integer** (the Jackson bracket): `qIntGeo`\naway from `q = 1`, and `n` at `q = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalCl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalCl","kind":"def","line":261,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L261-L264","doc":"**Classical-extended balanced q-integer**: `qIntBal` away from `q = 1`, and\nits removable-singularity value `n` at `q = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ_bar_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ_bar_even","kind":"theorem","line":202,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L202-L206","doc":"**The ℤ form is bar-even too** — the defining property survives the\nextension, so a signed index does not break the bar-parity bookkeeping. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ_zero","kind":"theorem","line":199,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L199-L200","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ_neg","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L193-L197","doc":"**Oddness in the index** — the fact the ℕ index cannot state, and the reason\nthis sibling exists. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ_natCast","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ_natCast","kind":"theorem","line":184,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L184-L191","doc":"**The compatibility theorem**: the ℤ form restricts to the ℕ form.\n\nThis is what makes `qIntBalZ` an *extension* of `qIntBal` rather than a rival\ndefinition, and it is the lemma a migration needs when a ℤ-indexed site meets an\nℕ-indexed lemma. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ_def","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ_def","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L179-L182","doc":"Defining equation, exposed for the same `simp`-after-migration reason as\n`qIntBal_def`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBalZ","kind":"def","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L170-L177","doc":"**The canonical quantum integer at integer index**,\n`[k]_q = (q^k − q^{−k})/(q − q⁻¹)` for `k : ℤ`.\n\nRestricts to `qIntBal` on `ℕ` (`qIntBalZ_natCast`), so this is one object with\n`qIntBal`, not a third convention.  Use it when the index is genuinely signed —\na shifted Hecke level `[k−3]_q`, or a weight running over `n−1, n−3, …, −(n−1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntGeo_bar_covariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntGeo_bar_covariant","kind":"theorem","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L147-L152","doc":"**The geometric form is bar-COVARIANT, not invariant** — weight `−(n−1)`.\nThis is the separating fact: it is why a geometric `[n]` cannot carry a bar-even\nquantity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_bar_even_canonical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_bar_even_canonical","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L142-L145","doc":"**The canonical form is bar-even** — the property that forces it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_eq_twist_canonical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_eq_twist_canonical","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L136-L140","doc":"**The twist**: the two conventions are one object, related by `q ↦ q²` and a\nmonomial.  Restated here so the bridge is visible from the canonical module. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntGeo_def","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntGeo_def","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L128-L130","doc":"Defining equation for the geometric form, same purpose. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_def","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBal_def","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L119-L126","doc":"**Defining equation**, exposed as `simp`/`rw` fodder.\n\nConsolidating a local duplicate onto `qIntBal` otherwise breaks every downstream\nproof that did `unfold <local>` — the unfold now stops at the alias.  With this\nlemma `simp [<local>]` reaches the formula again, so a migration needs no edits\nto existing proofs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntGeo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntGeo","kind":"def","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L112-L117","doc":"**The geometric quantum integer** `∑_{i<n} qⁱ = (qⁿ − 1)/(q − 1)`.\n\nCorrect **only where the variable means `q²`** (see `qIntBal_eq_twist_canonical`).\nIt is *not* bar-even, so it must not carry a bar-even quantity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QInteger.qIntBal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QInteger.qIntBal","kind":"def","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QIntegerCanonical.lean#L105-L110","doc":"**The canonical quantum integer** `[n]_q = (qⁿ − q⁻ⁿ)/(q − q⁻¹)`.\n\nBar-even, the `U_q(sl₂)` quantum dimension, and the form matching AGENTS.md §7a's\nHecke normalisation.  Use this unless your variable means `q²` — see `qIntGeo`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}